CAT = "rdFoodservice"; SEG = "FSV"
Q = [
("A standardized recipe yields 40 portions. The production sheet calls for 150 portions. What is the conversion factor?",
 "3.75", ["0.27", "2.75", "4.00"],
 "Conversion factor = desired yield / original yield = 150 / 40 = 3.75; every ingredient is multiplied by it. Dividing the other way (40 / 150) gives 0.27."),
("A recipe needs 36 lb of edible-portion carrots. The yield from as-purchased to edible portion is 80%. How much should be ordered?",
 "45 lb", ["28.8 lb", "43.2 lb", "38 lb"],
 "As-purchased weight = edible portion / yield = 36 / 0.80 = 45 lb. Multiplying by the yield (28.8 lb) would leave the kitchen short."),
("In one month, a café's food cost was $5,880 on food sales of $16,800. What was the food cost percentage?",
 "35%", ["65%", "29%", "40%"],
 "Food cost % = food cost / food sales x 100 = 5,880 / 16,800 x 100 = 35%. The remaining 65% of sales must cover labor, other costs and profit."),
("A kitchen used $31,500 of food in a month and its average inventory was $7,000. What is the inventory turnover rate?",
 "4.5", ["0.22", "3.5", "5.5"],
 "Inventory turnover = cost of food used / average inventory value = 31,500 / 7,000 = 4.5 times. A very low rate means money is tied up in stock; a very high one can mean frequent stockouts."),
("A #12 scoop holds about how much?",
 "1/3 cup", ["1/2 cup", "1/4 cup", "3/4 cup"],
 "The scoop number is how many level scoops make a quart. A quart is 4 cups, so a #12 holds 4 / 12 = 1/3 cup; a #8 holds 1/2 cup and a #16 holds 1/4 cup."),
("Over the last three Tuesdays a cafeteria sold 410, 360 and 440 chicken entrees. Using a simple moving average, what is the forecast for next Tuesday?",
 "About 403", ["About 360", "About 440", "About 1,210"],
 "A simple moving average adds the recent periods and divides by their number: (410 + 360 + 440) / 3 = 1,210 / 3 = 403. The total of 1,210 is three weeks of sales, not one."),
("A hospital rotates the same set of menus every 3 weeks. What type of menu is this?",
 "Cycle menu", ["Static menu", "Single-use menu", "Spoken menu"],
 "A cycle menu repeats a series of menus over a set period, which simplifies forecasting and purchasing while giving variety. A static menu offers the same items every day, as in a restaurant."),
("In a HACCP plan, the step where cooked chili is cooled before storage is set as a point where control prevents a hazard. What is it called?",
 "Critical control point", ["Critical limit point", "Corrective action step", "Verification step"],
 "A critical control point is a step where control is essential to prevent or reduce a hazard. Critical limits are the time and temperature values that must be met at that point."),
("Cooked stew must be cooled from 135 °F to 70 °F within what time under the FDA Food Code?",
 "2 hours", ["4 hours", "6 hours", "30 minutes"],
 "The Food Code two-stage method cools from 135 °F to 70 °F within 2 hours and then to 41 °F or below within the next 4 hours, 6 hours in all. Shallow pans and ice wands speed cooling."),
("Which purchasing method uses written specifications, sealed bids and a contract awarded to the lowest responsive bidder?",
 "Formal competitive bidding", ["Informal open-market buying", "Prime vendor agreement", "Cost-plus purchasing"],
 "Formal bidding is common in schools, government and large hospitals and depends on clear specifications. Informal buying compares quotes by phone or online for smaller purchases."),
("Which production system cooks food in bulk, chills it quickly and stores it for reheating days later?",
 "Cook-chill system", ["Conventional system", "Assembly-serve system", "Commissary hot-hold"],
 "Ready-prepared (cook-chill or cook-freeze) systems separate production from service, letting the kitchen produce on a schedule. Conventional systems cook close to service time."),
("A recipe calls for 350 °F in a standard oven. What setting is usually used in a convection oven?",
 "325 °F", ["350 °F", "375 °F", "400 °F"],
 "The fan in a convection oven moves hot air across the food and transfers heat faster, so the temperature is usually lowered by about 25 °F and the time checked early."),
("Which method lists the items to prepare, quantities, staff assigned and times for each shift?",
 "Production schedule", ["Standardized recipe", "Purchase specification", "Requisition form"],
 "A production schedule is the work order for a shift: menu items, amounts, who prepares them and when. A standardized recipe tells how to make one item."),
("Patient trays are plated in a central kitchen and taken to the floors on carts. What service system is this?",
 "Centralized tray service", ["Decentralized service", "Cafeteria-line service", "Family-style service"],
 "In centralized service trays are assembled in the main kitchen. In decentralized service food goes in bulk to floor pantries where trays are plated."),
("A school wants to know how much of each menu item students actually eat. Which method measures this?",
 "Plate waste study", ["Sales history review", "Taste panel survey", "Production forecast"],
 "A plate waste study weighs or estimates food left on trays to show acceptance and actual intake. Sales records show what was taken, not what was eaten."),
("Which inventory method keeps a running record of each item received and issued?",
 "Perpetual inventory", ["Physical inventory", "ABC classification", "Par stock count"],
 "A perpetual inventory updates the balance with every receipt and issue. A physical inventory is a periodic count of what is on the shelves, used to check the perpetual record."),
]
